 Conclusion","text":"Going beyond mere correlation, regression analysis allows us specify linear model form equation. Assuming conditions met, allows us say association. example, slope predicts response changes comparing two values predictor. fact, can use regression line make prediction reasonable value predictor (careful extrapolate). regression model, predictions exactly correct. Real data comes residuals, meaning deviations idealized predictions model. residuals relatively small \\(R^2\\) value large model good job making reasonably accurate predictions.","code":""},{"path":"regression.html","id":"regression-prep","chapter":"7 Regression","heading":"7.13.1 Preparing and submitting your assignment","text":"“Run” menu, select “Restart R Run Chunks”.Deal code errors crop . Repeat steps 1–-2 code errors.Spell check document clicking icon “ABC” check mark.Hit “Preview” button one last time generate final draft .nb.html file.Proofread HTML file carefully. errors, go back fix , repeat steps 1–5 .completed chapter part statistics course, follow directions receive professor submit assignment.","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","text":"\n2.0\n","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"functions-introduced-in-this-chapter-7","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Functions introduced in this chapter","text":"set.seed, rflip, ","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-intro","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.1 Introduction","text":"module, ’ll learn randomization simulation. want understand sampling works, ’s helpful simulate process drawing samples repeatedly population. days computing, difficult . Now, simple lines computer code can generate thousands (even millions) random samples, often matter seconds less.","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-install","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.1.1 Install new packages","text":"using RStudio Workbench, need install packages. (packages need already installed server administrators.)using R RStudio machine instead accessing RStudio Workbench browser, ’ll need type following command Console:","code":"install.packages(\"mosaic\")"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-download","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.1.2 Download the R notebook file","text":"Check upper-right corner RStudio make sure ’re intro_stats project. click following link download chapter R notebook file (.Rmd).https://vectorposse.github.io/intro_stats/chapter_downloads/08-intro_to_randomization_1.RmdOnce file downloaded, move project folder RStudio open .","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-restart","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.1.3 Restart R and run all chunks","text":"RStudio, select “Restart R Run Chunks” “Run” menu.","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-load","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.1.4 Load packages","text":"load tidyverse package. mosaic package contains tools making easier learn randomization simulation.","code":"\nlibrary(tidyverse)## ── Attaching core tidyverse packages ──────────────────────── tidyverse 2.0.0 ──\n## ✔ dplyr     1.1.2     ✔ readr     2.1.4\n## ✔ forcats   1.0.0     ✔ stringr   1.5.0\n## ✔ ggplot2   3.4.2     ✔ tibble    3.2.1\n## ✔ lubridate 1.9.2     ✔ tidyr     1.3.0\n## ✔ purrr     1.0.2     \n## ── Conflicts ────────────────────────────────────────── tidyverse_conflicts() ──\n## ✖ dplyr::filter() masks stats::filter()\n## ✖ dplyr::lag()    masks stats::lag()\n## ℹ Use the conflicted package (<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>) to force all conflicts to become errors\nlibrary(mosaic)## Warning: package 'mosaic' was built under R version 4.3.1## Registered S3 method overwritten by 'mosaic':\n##   method                           from   \n##   fortify.SpatialPolygonsDataFrame ggplot2\n## \n## The 'mosaic' package masks several functions from core packages in order to add \n## additional features.  The original behavior of these functions should not be affected by this.\n## \n## Attaching package: 'mosaic'\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:Matrix':\n## \n##     mean\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:dplyr':\n## \n##     count, do, tally\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:purrr':\n## \n##     cross\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:ggplot2':\n## \n##     stat\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':\n## \n##     binom.test, cor, cor.test, cov, fivenum, IQR, median, prop.test,\n##     quantile, sd, t.test, var\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:base':\n## \n##     max, mean, min, prod, range, sample, sum"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-sample-pop","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.2 Sample and population","text":"goal next chapters help think process sampling population. terms mean?population group objects like study. sounds vague, ’s . population can group size type thing ’re interested. Often, populations refer groups people. example, election, population interest voters. ’re biologist, might study populations kinds organisms. ’re engineer, might study populations bolts bridges. ’re finance, might study populations loans.Populations usually inaccessible entirety. impossible survey every voter reasonably sized election, example. Therefore, study , collect sample. sample subset population. might conduct poll 2000 voters try learn voting intentions entire population. course, work, sample representative population. ’ll say future.","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-coin","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.3 Flipping a coin","text":"talk samples obtained populations real world, ’re going perform simulations.One simplest acts simulate flipping coin. get actual coin physically flip , time-consuming annoying. much easier flip “virtual” coin inside computer. One way accomplish R use rflip command mosaic package. make sure ’re flipping fair coin, ’ll say want 50% chance heads including parameter prob = 0.5.One bit technical detail. Since randomness involved , need include R command ensure get results every time code runs. called “setting seed”. Don’t worry much hood. basic idea two people start seed generate sequence “random” numbers.seed 1234 chunk totally arbitrary. number . (, fact, ’ll use different numbers just fun.) change seed, get different output, need use seed. actual common value use seed irrelevant.one coin flip 50% chance coming heads:ten coin flips, 50% chance coming heads:Just confirm random process, let’s flip ten coins (without setting seed ):return previous seed 1234, obtain ten coin flips first:just see effect setting different seed:","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\nrflip(1, prob = 0.5)## \n## Flipping 1 coin [ Prob(Heads) = 0.5 ] ...\n## \n## T\n## \n## Number of Heads: 0 [Proportion Heads: 0]\nset.seed(1234)\nrflip(10, prob = 0.5)## \n## Flipping 10 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.5 ] ...\n## \n## T H H H H H T T H H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 7 [Proportion Heads: 0.7]\nrflip(10, prob = 0.5)## \n## Flipping 10 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.5 ] ...\n## \n## H H T H T H T T T T\n## \n## Number of Heads: 4 [Proportion Heads: 0.4]\nset.seed(1234)\nrflip(10, prob = 0.5)## \n## Flipping 10 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.5 ] ...\n## \n## T H H H H H T T H H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 7 [Proportion Heads: 0.7]\nset.seed(9999)\nrflip(10, prob = 0.5)## \n## Flipping 10 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.5 ] ...\n## \n## H H H T H H T H H H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 8 [Proportion Heads: 0.8]"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-1-5","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 1","text":"ten coin flips, many generally expect come heads? actual number heads saw simulations ? aren’t simulations coming expected number heads time?Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-multiple","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.4 Multiple simulations","text":"Suppose now person flipping coins. Suppose bunch people room flipping coins. ’ll start ten coin flips per person, task reasonably done even without computer.might observe three heads ten flips. Fine, everyone else room? numbers heads see?command mosaic way something multiple times. Imagine twenty people room, flipping coin ten times, time 50% probability coming heads. Observe:syntax simpler: (20) * means, literally, “twenty times.” words, command telling R repeat action twenty times, action flipping single coin ten times.’ll notice place list outcomes (H T) individual flips, instead summary number heads tails person sees. row represents person, columns give information person’s flips. (n = 10 flips person, number heads/tails—corresponding “proportion” heads—changes person person.)Looking rows columns, see output little coin-flipping experiment actually stored data frame! Let’s give name work .significant can store outcomes way. data frame, can apply data analysis tools (graphs, charts, tables, summary statistics, etc.) “data” generated set simulations.example, mean number heads twenty people observed?","code":"\nset.seed(12345)\ndo(20) * rflip(10, prob = 0.5)\nset.seed(12345)\ncoin_flips_20_10 <- do(20) * rflip(10, prob = 0.5)\ncoin_flips_20_10\nmean(coin_flips_20_10$heads)## [1] 5.6"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-2-4","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 2","text":"data frame coin_flips_20_10 contains four variables: n, heads, tails, prop. code chunk , calculated mean(coin_flips_20_10$heads) gave us mean count heads people flipping coins. Instead calculating mean count heads, change variable heads prop calculate mean proportion heads. explain answer makes sense light mean count heads calculated .Please write answer .Let’s look histogram number heads see simulated flips. (fancy stuff scale_x_continuous just making sure x-axis goes 0 10 tick marks appear whole number.)Let’s thing, now let’s consider proportion heads.","code":"\n# Add code here to calculate the mean proportion of heads.\nggplot(coin_flips_20_10, aes(x = heads)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.5) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(-1, 11), breaks = seq(0, 10, 1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`).\nggplot(coin_flips_20_10, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.05) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(-0.1, 1.1), breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`)."},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-bigger","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.5 Bigger and better!","text":"twenty people, possible , example, nobody get heads tails. Indeed, coin_flips_20_10 people got heads tails. Also, people six seven heads five heads, even though “expected” average five heads. nothing particularly significant ; happened pure chance alone. Another run commands generate somewhat different outcome. ’s happens things random.Instead, let’s imagine recruited way people flip coins us. Let’s try 2000 people:idea , now 2000 rows data frame instead 20.helpful. contrast set simulations twenty people, last histogram gives us something closer expect. mode five heads, every possible number heads represented, decreasing counts one moves away five. 2000 people flipping coins, possible outcomes—including rare ones—better represented.histogram, time proportion heads instead count heads:","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\ncoin_flips_2000_10 <- do(2000) * rflip(10, prob = 0.5)\ncoin_flips_2000_10\nmean(coin_flips_2000_10$heads)## [1] 5.0245\nggplot(coin_flips_2000_10, aes(x = heads)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.5) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(-1, 11), breaks = seq(0, 10, 1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`).\nggplot(coin_flips_2000_10, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.05) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(-0.1, 1.1), breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`)."},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-3-4","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 3","text":"think shape distribution appreciably different used 20,000 even 200,000 people? ? (Normally, encourage test theory trying R. However, takes long time simulate many flips don’t want tie resources memory. Think head.)Please write answer .now , insist using least thousand simulations——make sure represent full range possible outcomes.10","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-more","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.6 More flips","text":"Now let’s increase number coin flips person performs. ’ll still use 2000 simulations (imagine 2000 people flipping coins), time, person flip coin 1000 times instead 10 times. first code chunk accounts substantial amount time takes run code document.now histogram, proportions:","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\ncoin_flips_2000_1000 <- do(2000) * rflip(1000, prob = 0.5)\ncoin_flips_2000_1000\nmean(coin_flips_2000_1000$heads)## [1] 499.9055\nggplot(coin_flips_2000_1000, aes(x = heads)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 10, boundary = 500)\nggplot(coin_flips_2000_1000, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.01, boundary = 0.5)"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-4-4","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 4","text":"Comment histogram . Describe shape using vocabulary three important features (modes, symmetry, outliers). think ’s shaped like ?Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-5-4","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 5","text":"Given amount randomness involved (person tossing coins randomly come heads tails), see much structure orderliness histograms?Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"randomization1-who-cares","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"8.7 But who cares about coin flips?","text":"’s fair ask go trouble talk coin flips. pressing research questions day involve people sitting around flipping coins, either physically virtually.now substitute “heads” “tails” “cancer” “cancer”. “guilty” “guilty”. “shot” “shot”. fact many important issues measured variables two possible outcomes. underlying “probability” seeing one outcome . (doesn’t 50% like coin.) Statistical methods—including simulation—can say lot “expect” see outcomes truly random. importantly, see outcomes aren’t consistent simulations, may wonder underlying mechanism may random . may look like first blush, idea core scientific method.example, let’s suppose 85% U.S. adults support form background checks gun buyers.11 Now, imagine went surveyed random group people asked simple yes/question support background checks. might see?Let’s simulate. Imagine flipping coin, instead coming heads 50% time, suppose possible coin come heads 85% time.12 sequence heads tails weird coin much like randomly surveying people asking background checks.can make “virtual” weird coin rflip command specifying often want heads come .flip weird coin bunch times, can see coin fair. Indeed, appears come heads way often :results code can thought survey 100 random U.S. adults support background checks purchasing guns. “Heads” means “supports” “tails” means “opposes.” majority Americans support background checks, come across people survey tell us support background checks. shows simulation appearance heads tails.Note guarantee sample exactly 85% heads. fact, doesn’t; 90% heads., keep mind ’re simulating act obtaining random sample 100 U.S. adults. get different sample, ’ll get different results. (set different seed . ensures code chunk randomly different one .)See, time, 81% came heads, even though expected 85%. ’s randomness works.","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\nrflip(1, prob = 0.85)## \n## Flipping 1 coin [ Prob(Heads) = 0.85 ] ...\n## \n## H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 1 [Proportion Heads: 1]\nset.seed(1234)\nrflip(100, prob = 0.85)## \n## Flipping 100 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.85 ] ...\n## \n## H H H H T H H H H H H H H T H H H H H H H H H H H H H T H H H H H H H H\n## H H T H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H T H H H H H H H H H H T\n## H H H H H H H H T H H H H T H H H T H T H H H H H H H H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 90 [Proportion Heads: 0.9]\nset.seed(123456)\nrflip(100, prob = 0.85)## \n## Flipping 100 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.85 ] ...\n## \n## H H H H H H H H T H H H T T T T T H H H H H H H H H T T T H H T H H H H\n## T T H H H H T H H H H H H H H H H T H T H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H H\n## T H H H T H H H H H H T H H H H H H H H H H H H T H H H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 81 [Proportion Heads: 0.81]"},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-6a-2","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 6(a)","text":"Now imagine 2000 people go conduct surveys 100 random U.S. adults, asking support background checks. Write R code simulates . Plot histogram results. (Hint: ’ll need (2000) * .) Use proportion supporters (prop), raw count supporters (heads).","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\n# Add code here to simulate 2000 surveys of 100 U.S. adults.\n# Plot the results in a histogram using proportions."},{"path":"randomization1.html","id":"exercise-6b-2","chapter":"8 Introduction to randomization, Part 1","heading":"Exercise 6(b)","text":"Run another simulation, time, person survey 1000 adults just 100.","code":"\nset.seed(1234)\n# Add code here to simulate 2000 surveys of 1000 U.S. adults.\n# Plot the results in a histogram using proportions."},