ublic  public  public  public  public  public  private public  public \n## [190] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n## [199] public  public \n## Levels: public private\ntabyl(sample_with_replacement1)\nset.seed(11)\nsample_with_replacement2 <- sample(hsb2$schtyp, size = 200, replace = TRUE)\nsample_with_replacement2##   [1] public  public  public  public  public  private public  public  private\n##  [10] public  public  private public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [19] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [28] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [37] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [46] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  private public \n##  [55] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [64] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n##  [73] public  public  public  public  private public  public  public  public \n##  [82] public  private private public  public  private public  public  public \n##  [91] public  private public  public  public  public  private public  private\n## [100] public  public  private public  public  public  public  public  public \n## [109] public  public  private public  public  private public  public  public \n## [118] private private public  public  public  public  private public  private\n## [127] public  private public  private public  public  public  private public \n## [136] private public  public  public  private private private public  private\n## [145] public  public  private public  public  private public  public  public \n## [154] private private public  public  public  public  public  public  private\n## [163] private public  public  public  public  public  private public  private\n## [172] public  public  public  private private public  private public  public \n## [181] public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public  public \n## [190] public  private public  private public  public  public  private public \n## [199] public  public \n## Levels: public private\ntabyl(sample_with_replacement2)"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-computing-boot","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.4 Computing a bootstrap sampling distribution","text":"infer package can compute bootstrap samples , hence, produce bootstrap sampling distribution. code looks whole like code already know hypothesis testing:simply changed type “bootstrap”.Now visualize like normal:(can change number bins want, number looks pretty good.)","code":"\nprivate_boot <- hsb2 %>%\n    specify(response = schtyp, success = \"private\") %>%\n    generate(reps = 1000, type = \"bootstrap\") %>%\n    calculate(stat = \"prop\")\nprivate_boot\nprivate_boot %>%\n    visualize()"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ci","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.5 Confidence intervals","text":"histogram simulates might happen took many samples infinite “fake” population consisting many copies original, actual sample data. lower end, might see something like 8% private school students. upper end, see 25% private school students.chapter numerical data, computed IQR (interquartile range), difference 25th percentile 75th percentile. IQR range middle 50% data. Let’s use infer tools calculate middle 50% distribution:middle 50% ranges 14% 17.5%. can also visualize :words, go gather sample (fake infinite) population high school seniors, half time, expect percentage private students somewhere 14% 17.5%. half time, sample value outside range.confidence interval. specifically, 50% confidence interval. range values expect sample proportions approximately half samples might gather (fake infinite) population.Now don’t forget goal. really trying find value \\(p\\), true population parameter. want know proportion high school seniors attend private school whole population high school seniors U.S.mathematical reasons outside scope course, turns sampling variability bootstrap distribution around \\(\\hat{p}\\) similar sampling variability sample proportion \\(\\hat{p}\\) around true value \\(p\\). bootstrapped way picture using one actual sample 16% private school students. different sample high school seniors give us different bootstrap samples, producing slightly different bootstrap distribution one . , , shaded region like histogram . Every actual sample might obtain real world give us bootstrap distribution different shaded region. amazing fact : half shaded regions actually contain true population parameter \\(p\\).Think value \\(p\\) like fish hidden murky lake. sample proportion \\(\\hat{p}\\) attempt fishing. drop hook value \\(\\hat{p}\\) pull right back . ’s likely caught fish, although hope close. Alas, sample proportion almost never exactly equal true proportion \\(p\\). cast net instead? net shaded range values confidence interval. range values might catch fish.difference statistics fishing , latter, pull net, can see successfully caught fish. former, can say probability net caught fish, ’re able look inside net know sure.confidence interval created might caught true value \\(p\\). , might . ’s 50% chance captured true value range 14% 17.5% computed specific sample accompanying bootstrap samples. researchers displeased 50% success rate. can better?much better want ? subjective question definitive answer. Many people say want 95% confident confidence interval build capture true population parameter. Let’s modify code :middle 95% ranges 11% 21.5%. can also visualize :interpretation go collect many samples, confidence intervals produce using bootstrap procedure described capture true population proportion 95% time.","code":"\nprivate_50 <- private_boot %>%\n    get_confidence_interval(level = 0.5)\nprivate_50\nprivate_boot %>%\n    visualise() +\n    shade_confidence_interval(endpoints = private_50)\nprivate_95 <- private_boot %>%\n    get_confidence_interval(level = 0.95)\nprivate_95\nprivate_boot %>%\n    visualise() +\n    shade_confidence_interval(endpoints = private_95)"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"exercise-1-9","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"Exercise 1","text":"95% confidence interval wider 50% confidence interval? words, desire 95% confident capturing true value \\(p\\) result interval wider wanted 50% confident?Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"exercise-2-6","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"Exercise 2","text":"confident seems like good thing. fact, might want 99% confidence interval. Compute visualize 99% confidence interval proportion private school students.","code":"\n# Add code here to compute a 99% confidence interval\n# Add code here to visualize a 99% confidence interval"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"exercise-3-7","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"Exercise 3","text":"Can think downside using higher higher confidence levels? hint, think following completely true sentence: “100% confident true proportion high school seniors attending private school somewhere 0% 100%.”Please write answer .50% clearly low confidence level, seen , particular reason need compute 95% confidence interval either. consensus scientific community : 95% evolved become generally agreed-upon standard. compute 90% confidence interval 99% confidence interval (), type interval. said , choose intervals besides three, people might wonder ’re something.15","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-conditions","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.6 Conditions","text":"Don’t forget always assumptions make relying kind statistical inference. computing confidence interval proportion, must verify certain conditions satisfied. conditions new. already know hypothesis testing required good inference sample. “Random” “10%” conditions.Random\nsample must random (hopefully representative).\nsample must random (hopefully representative).10%\nsample size must less 10% size population.\nsample size must less 10% size population.conditions met data High School Beyond survey.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-rubric","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.7 Rubric for confidence intervals","text":"Typically, asked report confidence interval performing hypothesis test. Whereas hypothesis test gives “decision criterion” (using data make decision reject null fail reject null), confidence interval gives estimate “effect size” (range plausible values population parameter)., section Rubric inference shows steps calculating reporting confidence interval. follows:Check relevant conditions ensure model assumptions met.Calculate graph confidence interval.State (overstate) contextually meaningful interpretation.running two-sided test, explain confidence interval reinforces conclusion hypothesis test.comparing two groups, comment effect size practical significance result.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8 Example","text":"worked example. (Unless otherwise stated, always use 95% confidence level.)students “High School Beyond” survey attended vocational programs. data stored prog variable. Using confidence interval, estimate percentage high school seniors attend vocational programs.need little data cleaning can address question. actually three types programs: “general”, “academic”, “vocational”. infer commands work categorical variable two levels. thinking “general” “academic” together like combined “” category. can fix creating new factor variable mutate. Inside mutate, use fct_collapse function collapse two levels one follows:Inspect variables prog prog2 make sure recoding successful. sure use prog2 prog everywhere.","code":"\nhsb2 <- hsb2 %>%\n    mutate(prog2 = fct_collapse(prog,\n                                vocational = \"vocational\",\n                                other = c(\"general\", \"academic\")))\nglimpse(hsb2)## Rows: 200\n## Columns: 12\n## $ id      <int> 70, 121, 86, 141, 172, 113, 50, 11, 84, 48, 75, 60, 95, 104, 3…\n## $ gender  <chr> \"male\", \"female\", \"male\", \"male\", \"male\", \"male\", \"male\", \"mal…\n## $ race    <chr> \"white\", \"white\", \"white\", \"white\", \"white\", \"white\", \"african…\n## $ ses     <fct> low, middle, high, high, middle, middle, middle, middle, middl…\n## $ schtyp  <fct> public, public, public, public, public, public, public, public…\n## $ prog    <fct> general, vocational, general, vocational, academic, academic, …\n## $ read    <int> 57, 68, 44, 63, 47, 44, 50, 34, 63, 57, 60, 57, 73, 54, 45, 42…\n## $ write   <int> 52, 59, 33, 44, 52, 52, 59, 46, 57, 55, 46, 65, 60, 63, 57, 49…\n## $ math    <int> 41, 53, 54, 47, 57, 51, 42, 45, 54, 52, 51, 51, 71, 57, 50, 43…\n## $ science <int> 47, 63, 58, 53, 53, 63, 53, 39, 58, 50, 53, 63, 61, 55, 31, 50…\n## $ socst   <int> 57, 61, 31, 56, 61, 61, 61, 36, 51, 51, 61, 61, 71, 46, 56, 56…\n## $ prog2   <fct> other, vocational, other, vocational, other, other, other, oth…"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex-conditions","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8.1 Check the relevant conditions to ensure that model assumptions are met.","text":"Random\nsample random sample high school seniors U.S. survey conducted National Center Education Statistics, reputable government organization.\nsample random sample high school seniors U.S. survey conducted National Center Education Statistics, reputable government organization.10%\nsample size 200, much less 10% population U.S. high school seniors.\nsample size 200, much less 10% population U.S. high school seniors.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex-calcaulte","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8.2 Calculate and graph the confidence interval.","text":"","code":"\nvocational_boot <- hsb2 %>%\n    specify(response = prog2, success = \"vocational\") %>%\n    generate(reps = 1000, type = \"bootstrap\") %>%\n    calculate(stat = \"prop\")\nvocational_boot\nvocational_ci <- vocational_boot %>%\n    get_confidence_interval(level = 0.95)\nvocational_ci\nvocational_boot %>%\n    visualize() +\n    shade_confidence_interval(endpoints = vocational_ci)"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex-interpret","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8.3 State (but do not overstate) a contextually meaningful interpretation.","text":"95% confident true percentage U.S. high school seniors attend vocational program captured interval (19%, 31%).Note: use inline code grab values endpoints confidence interval. also multiply 100 report percentages instead proportions.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex-ci-ht","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8.4 If running a two-sided test, explain how the confidence interval reinforces the conclusion of the hypothesis test.","text":"chapter, haven’t run hypothesis test, step irrelevant us . However, future chapters, incorporate step rubric see confidence interval relates conclusion hypothesis test.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-ex-effect","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.8.5 When comparing two groups, comment on the effect size and the practical significance of the result.","text":"step also become clear future chapters. applies situations attempting find difference two groups. example, ’re simply using sample statistic estimate single population parameter.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-your-turn","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.9 Your turn","text":"Use smoking data set openintro package. percentage population U.K. smokes tobacco? (information need smoke variable.) Use 95% confidence interval.","code":""},{},{},{},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-interpret","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.10 Interpreting confidence intervals","text":"Confidence intervals notoriously difficult interpret.16Here several wrong interpretations 95% confidence interval:95% data lies interval.95% data lies interval.95% chance sample proportion lies interval.95% chance sample proportion lies interval.95% chance population parameter lies interval.95% chance population parameter lies interval.’ll take closer look incorrect claims moment. First, let’s see confidence intervals work using simulation.order simulate, ’ll pretend temporarily know true population parameter. Let’s use example candidate support 64% voters. words, \\(p = 0.64\\). go get sample voters, let’s say 50. sample construct 95% confidence interval bootstrapping. time, 64% (true value!) interval. sometimes won’t . can get unusual sample far away 64%, just pure chance alone. (Perhaps accidentally run bunch people oppose candidate.)Okay, let’s . Get new sample calculate new confidence interval. sample likely result different sample proportion first sample. Therefore, confidence interval located different place. contain 64%? time, expect . Occasionally, .can magic simulation! ’s simulation looks like R. following code quite technical, although recognize bits pieces . Don’t worry . won’t need generate code like . Just look pretty picture output code.sample gives us slightly different estimate, therefore, different confidence interval well.100 simulated intervals, (black ones) capture true value 0.64 (blue vertical line). Occasionally don’t (red ones). expect 5 red intervals, since randomness involved, won’t necessarily exactly 5. (3 bad intervals.)key interpreting confidence intervals. “95%” 95% confidence interval means collect many random samples, 95% contain true population parameter 5% .let’s revisit erroneous statements beginning section correct misconceptions.95% data lies interval.\ndoesn’t even make sense. data categorical. confidence interval range plausible values proportion successes sample.\ndoesn’t even make sense. data categorical. confidence interval range plausible values proportion successes sample.95% chance sample proportion lies interval.\n. essentially 100% chance sample proportion lies interval. time, sample proportion close center interval. bootstrap, “infinite population” simulating population proportion sample started . (, infinite population just many copies sample started .) Therefore, samples infinite population less centered around sample proportion.\n. essentially 100% chance sample proportion lies interval. time, sample proportion close center interval. bootstrap, “infinite population” simulating population proportion sample started . (, infinite population just many copies sample started .) Therefore, samples infinite population less centered around sample proportion.95% chance population parameter lies interval.\nwrong subtle way. problem takes interval fixed special, tries declare possible population parameters, 95% chance true one landing interval. logic backwards. population parameter fixed truth. doesn’t wander around land interval sometimes times. confidence interval wanders; just one many intervals obtained random sampling. say, “95% confident …,” just using convenient shorthand , “repeat process sampling creating confidence intervals many times, 95% times produce interval happens capture actual population proportion.” ’re lazy don’t want say every time.\nwrong subtle way. problem takes interval fixed special, tries declare possible population parameters, 95% chance true one landing interval. logic backwards. population parameter fixed truth. doesn’t wander around land interval sometimes times. confidence interval wanders; just one many intervals obtained random sampling. say, “95% confident …,” just using convenient shorthand , “repeat process sampling creating confidence intervals many times, 95% times produce interval happens capture actual population proportion.” ’re lazy don’t want say every time.","code":"\nset.seed(11111)\n\n# The true population proportion is 0.64\ntrue_val <- 0.64\n# The sample size is 50\nsample_size <- 50\n# Set confidence level\nour_level <- 0.95\n# Set number of intervals to simulate\nsim_num <- 100\n\n# Get a random sample of size n.\n# Compute the test statistic and the bootstrap confidence interval.\n# Put both into a single tibble.\nsimulate_ci <- function(n, level = 0.95) {\n    sample_data <-\n        factor(rbinom(n , size = 1, prob = true_val)) %>%\n        tibble(data = .)\n    stat <- sample_data %>%\n        observe(response = data, success = \"1\", stat = \"prop\")\n    ci <- sample_data %>%\n        specify(response = data, success = \"1\") %>%\n        generate(reps = 1000, type = \"bootstrap\") %>%\n        calculate(stat = \"prop\") %>%\n        get_confidence_interval(level = our_level)\n    bind_cols(stat, ci) %>%\n        return()\n}\n\n# Simulate 100 random samples (each of size 50)\n# Assign a color based on whether the intervals contain the true proportion\nci <- map_dfr(rep(sample_size, times = sim_num), simulate_ci, level = our_level) %>%\n    mutate(row_num = row_number()) %>%\n    mutate(color = ifelse(lower_ci <= true_val & true_val <= upper_ci,\n                          \"black\", \"red\"),\n           alpha = ifelse(color == \"black\", 0.5, 1))\n\n# Plot all the simulated intervals\nggplot(ci, aes(x = stat, y = row_num,\n                   color = color, alpha = alpha)) +\n    geom_point() +\n    scale_color_manual(values = c(\"black\", \"red\"), guide = \"none\") +\n    geom_segment(aes(x = lower_ci, xend = upper_ci, yend = row_num)) +\n    geom_vline(xintercept = true_val, color = \"blue\") +\n    scale_alpha_identity() +\n    labs(y = \"Simulation\", x = \"Estimates with confidence intervals\")"},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-conclusion","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.11 Conclusion","text":"confidence interval form statistical inference gives us range numbers hope capture true population parameter. course, can’t certain . repeatedly collect samples, expectation 95% samples produce confidence intervals capture true population parameter, also means 5% . ’ll never know sample one 95% worked, one 5% . even get one intervals worked, range values ’s impossible determine values true population parameter. ’s statistics, just live uncertainty.","code":""},{"path":"ci.html","id":"ci-prep","chapter":"12 Confidence intervals","heading":"12.11.1 Preparing and submitting your assignment","text":"“Run” menu, select “Restart R Run Chunks”.Deal code errors crop . Repeat steps 1–-2 code errors.Spell check document clicking icon “ABC” check mark.Hit “Preview” button one last time generate final draft .nb.html file.Proofread HTML file carefully. errors, go back fix , repeat steps 1–5 .completed chapter part statistics course, follow directions receive professor submit assignment.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13 Normal models","text":"\n2.0\n","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"functions-introduced-in-this-chapter-12","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Functions introduced in this chapter","text":"pdist, diff, qdist, scale, geom_qq","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-intro","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.1 Introduction","text":"chapter learn work normal models. addition learning theoretical normal distributions, also develop QQ plots assess normality data.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-install","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.1.1 Install new packages","text":"new packages used chapter.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-download","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.1.2 Download the R notebook file","text":"Check upper-right corner RStudio make sure ’re intro_stats project. click following link download chapter R notebook file (.Rmd).https://vectorposse.github.io/intro_stats/chapter_downloads/13-normal_models.RmdOnce file downloaded, move project folder RStudio open .","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-restart","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.1.3 Restart R and run all chunks","text":"RStudio, select “Restart R Run Chunks” “Run” menu.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-load","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.2 Load packages","text":"addition tidyverse, return mosaic package produce nice visualizations normal models.","code":"\nlibrary(tidyverse)## ── Attaching core tidyverse packages ──────────────────────── tidyverse 2.0.0 ──\n## ✔ dplyr     1.1.2     ✔ readr     2.1.4\n## ✔ forcats   1.0.0     ✔ stringr   1.5.0\n## ✔ ggplot2   3.4.2     ✔ tibble    3.2.1\n## ✔ lubridate 1.9.2     ✔ tidyr     1.3.0\n## ✔ purrr     1.0.2     \n## ── Conflicts ────────────────────────────────────────── tidyverse_conflicts() ──\n## ✖ dplyr::filter() masks stats::filter()\n## ✖ dplyr::lag()    masks stats::lag()\n## ℹ Use the conflicted package (<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>) to force all conflicts to become errors\nlibrary(mosaic)## Warning: package 'mosaic' was built under R version 4.3.1## Registered S3 method overwritten by 'mosaic':\n##   method                           from   \n##   fortify.SpatialPolygonsDataFrame ggplot2\n## \n## The 'mosaic' package masks several functions from core packages in order to add \n## additional features.  The original behavior of these functions should not be affected by this.\n## \n## Attaching package: 'mosaic'\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:Matrix':\n## \n##     mean\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:dplyr':\n## \n##     count, do, tally\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:purrr':\n## \n##     cross\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:ggplot2':\n## \n##     stat\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':\n## \n##     binom.test, cor, cor.test, cov, fivenum, IQR, median, prop.test,\n##     quantile, sd, t.test, var\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:base':\n## \n##     max, mean, min, prod, range, sample, sum"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-clt","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.3 The Central Limit Theorem","text":"important aspect simulations ’ve done far—assuming ’ve run large enough number —histograms look like bell curves. fact known “Central Limit Theorem”. basic assumptions ’ll discuss later chapter, typical many simulated null distributions.rather running simulation time want conduct hypothesis test, also assume null distribution bell curve. rest chapter teach work “normal distribution,” just mathematically correct term bell curve.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-normal","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.4 Normal models","text":"normal distribution looks like :curve pictured called standard normal distribution. mean 0 standard deviation 1. Mathematically, written \\[\nN(\\mu = 0, \\sigma = 1),\n\\]usually just\\[\nN(0, 1).\n\\]use bell curve shape model data unimodal, symmetric, without outliers. statistical “model” simplification idealization. Reality , course, never perfectly bell-shaped. Real data exactly symmetric one clear peak middle. Nevertheless, abstract model can give us good answers used properly.example , systolic blood pressure (SBP, measured millimeters mercury, mmHg) --less normally distributed women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada, mean 114 standard deviation 14.17If plot histogram SBP every woman ages 30 44 U.S. Canada, shape normal distribution, instead centered 0 like graph , one centered 114. Mathematically, write\\[\nN(\\mu = 114, \\sigma = 14),\n\\]\\[\nN(114, 14).\n\\]graph now looks like :","code":"\n# Don't worry about the syntax here.\n# You won't need to know how to do this on your own.\nggplot(data.frame(x = c(-4, 4)), aes(x)) +\n    stat_function(fun = dnorm) +\n    scale_x_continuous(breaks = -3:3)\n# Again, don't worry about the syntax here.\nggplot(data.frame(x = c(58, 170)), aes(x)) +\n    stat_function(fun = dnorm, args = list(mean = 114, sd = 14)) +\n    scale_x_continuous(breaks = c(72, 86, 100, 114, 128, 142, 156))"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-predictions","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.5 Predictions using normal models","text":"Using information, can estimate percentage women expected range SBP without access data.example, percentage women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada expected SBP 130 mmHg? pdist command mosaic package help us calculation, also offers nice visual representation depending arguments supply function:notebook view, switch back forth two boxes code chunk (graph) see number versus graph. HTML output, however, number plot visible.situations really just want see number, can always add plot = FALSE function:pieces pdist function pretty intuitive: \"norm\" (quotes) indicates want normal model, q value interest us, mean sd self-evident. numerical output gives area curve left value interest. area 0.873451; words, 87.3% women expected SBP less 130.use command inline, pretty picture generated, just value. example, look following sentence (remembering can click anywhere inside inline R code hit Ctrl-Enter Cmd-Enter):model predicts 87.3451046% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada systolic blood pressure 130 mmHg.Note code multiplied result pdist command 100. important full sentence interpretation meant read human beings, human beings tend report kinds numbers percentages decimals.18It’s also important include phrase, “model predicts…” something like . Without part, claim likely false. definitive. Remember model just approximation simplification reality. ’re claiming ’ve found “True” number. know model roughly correct, can predict true value.’s another question: many women predicted SBP greater 130? 87.3% women SBP 130, 12.7% must SBP 130. ? women add 100%!Therefore, solve problem subtract number obtained previous question 1. (Remember 1 = 100%.)model predicts 12.6548954% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada systolic blood pressure 130 mmHg.Don’t forget include parentheses. need multiply whole expression 100.Now, ’s complicated question: percentage women predicted SBP 110 mmHg 130 mmHg?Recall proportion women predicted SBP less 130 mmHg 0.873. also counting women SBP 110 mmHg, now want exclude. proportion women SBP 110 found following code:Therefore, calculate 0.873 minus 0.388:model predicts 48.5902564% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada systolic blood pressure 110 mmHg 130 mmHg.(, don’t forget parentheses.)pretty picture? Unfortunately, doesn’t work well:code bulky prints two pictures, neither quite right question.Instead, let’s observe pdist command can include values (110 130) using vector notation c:Now picture looks great can see proportion desire area two lines 110 130.doesn’t work well numerical output though. Observe:two numbers shown, neither correct answer. command shows percentages 110 130, respectively, area 110 130. still subtract. However, R can us easily diff command:, inline R code, don’t need specify plot = FALSE:model predicts 48.5902564% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada systolic blood pressure 110 mmHg 130 mmHg.following exercises, ’ll use running example IQ scores. Keep mind , best, IQ scores fail measure anything like “intelligence” (https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2012/12/121219133334.htm). worse, IQ tests (forms standardized testing) used perpetuate systemic racism inequality (https://www.nea.org/advocating--change/new--nea/racist-beginnings-standardized-testing).IQ scores—whatever actually measure—standardized mean 100 standard deviation 16. exercise, use pdist draw right picture state answer contextually meaningful full sentence using inline R code. Don’t forget use phrase “model predicts…” report numbers percentages, decimals.","code":"\npdist(\"norm\", q = 130, mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 0.873451\npdist(\"norm\", q = 130, mean = 114, sd = 14, plot = FALSE)## [1] 0.873451\npdist(\"norm\", q = 110, mean = 114, sd = 14, plot = FALSE)## [1] 0.3875485\npdist(\"norm\", q = 130, mean = 114, sd = 14) - \n    pdist(\"norm\", q = 110, mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 0.4859026\npdist(\"norm\", q = c(110, 130), mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 0.3875485 0.8734510\npdist(\"norm\", q = c(110, 130), mean = 114, sd = 14, plot = FALSE)## [1] 0.3875485 0.8734510\npdist(\"norm\", q = c(110, 130), mean = 114, sd = 14, plot = FALSE) %>%\n    diff()## [1] 0.4859026"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-1a","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 1(a)","text":"percentage people expect IQ scores 80?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-1b","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 1(b)","text":"percentage people expect IQ scores 90?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-1c","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 1(c)","text":"percentage people expect IQ scores 112 132?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-percentiles","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.6 Percentiles","text":"Often, question reversed: instead getting value asked percentage population falls , given percentile asked value corresponds.example using systolic blood pressure: cutoff value SBP lowest 25% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada? words, 25th percentile SBP group women?command need qdist. looks lot like pdist. Observe:change one arguments p instead q, value p proportion (0 1) instead value SBP. output now SBP value.inline:model predicts 25th percentile SBP women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada 104.5571435 mmHg.asked highest 10% women? remember top 10% actually 90th percentile.model predicts top 10% SBP women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada SBP higher 131.9417219 mmHg.Finally, want middle 50%? trickier. middle 50% lies 25th percentile 75th percentile. Observe syntax :Therefore, model predicts middle 50% SBP women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada lies 104.5571435 mmHg 123.4428565 mmHg.something tricky inline code . qdist command produces two values (one 25th percentile one 75th percentile), can grab value separately appending [1] [2] end command.exercises , ’ll continue use IQ scores (mean 100 standard deviation 16). Use qdist command draw right picture state answer contextually meaningful full sentence. Don’t forget use phrase “model predicts…”","code":"\nqdist(\"norm\", p = 0.25, mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 104.5571\nqdist(\"norm\", p = 0.9, mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 131.9417\nqdist(\"norm\", p = c(0.25, 0.75), mean = 114, sd = 14)## [1] 104.5571 123.4429"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-2a-2","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 2(a)","text":"cutoff value bounds highest 5% IQ scores?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-2b-2","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 2(b)","text":"cutoff value bounds lowest 30% IQ scores?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-2c-2","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 2(c)","text":"cutoff values bound middle 80% IQ scores?Please write answer .","code":"\n# Add code here to draw the model."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-z","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.7 Z scores","text":"Sometimes easier refer value terms many standard deviations lies mean. example, systolic blood pressure 100 14 mmHg mean, since standard deviation 14 mmHg, means 100 one standard deviation mean. distance mean terms standard deviations called z score.calculate z scores using following formula:\\[\nz = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}.\n\\]example, wanted know z score SBP 100, just plug numbers formula :\\[\nz = \\frac{100 - 114}{14} = -1.\n\\]z score SBP 132? Look graph normal model \\(N(114, 14)\\):can see 132 lies 128 142, 1 2 standard deviations mean, respectively. exact z score \\[\nz = \\frac{132 - 114}{14} = 1.285714.\n\\]scale function R also computes z scores. Just note function takes arguments center scale, mean sd.Also note function spits bunch extra crap don’t care . goes away inline code. Go ahead preview HTML file now can see effect following sentence:z score 100 -1 z score 132 1.2857143.","code":"\n# Don't worry about the syntax here.\n# You won't need to know how to do this on your own.\nggplot(data.frame(x = c(58, 170)), aes(x)) +\n    stat_function(fun = dnorm, args = list(mean = 114, sd = 14)) +\n    scale_x_continuous(breaks = c(72, 86, 100, 114, 128, 142, 156)) +\n    geom_vline(xintercept = 132, color = \"blue\")\nscale(x = 100, center = 114, scale = 14)##      [,1]\n## [1,]   -1\n## attr(,\"scaled:center\")\n## [1] 114\n## attr(,\"scaled:scale\")\n## [1] 14\nscale(x = 132, center = 114, scale = 14)##          [,1]\n## [1,] 1.285714\n## attr(,\"scaled:center\")\n## [1] 114\n## attr(,\"scaled:scale\")\n## [1] 14"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-3-8","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 3","text":"IQ scores mean 100 standard deviation 16, z scores following IQ scores? Write answers full sentences using inline R code.80Please write answer .102Please write answer .130Please write answer .Working z scores also makes easier work normal models. default settings pdist qdist mean = 0 sd = 1. saves typing. , example, calculated SBP 100 z score -1. percentage women expected SBP lower 100?model predicts 15.8655254% women ages 30–44 U.S. Canada SBP less 100.","code":"\npdist(\"norm\", q = -1)## [1] 0.1586553"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-4-7","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 4","text":"Albert Einstein supposedly IQ 160. Calculate z score IQ use z score figure percentage population predicted higher IQ Einstein. Use full sentences inline R code express answer.Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-qq","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.8 QQ plots","text":"work normal models assumes normal model appropriate. want summarize data using normal model, means data distribution reasonably unimodal, symmetric, serious outliers.can, course, use histogram check . histogram can highly sensitive choice bins. Furthermore, small sample sizes, histograms look “chunky”, making hard test assumption.easier way check normality use quantile-quantile plot, typically called QQ plot sometimes normal probability plot. won’t get technicalities plot works. Suffice say data normally distributed, points QQ plot lie along diagonal line.example. total snowfall Grand Rapids, Michigan recorded every year since 1893. data included mosaic package data frame SnowGR. histogram (reasonable binning) shows data nearly normal.QQ plot data. Notice aesthetics little different; instead x, use sample.(warning one missing value data.)geom_qq() layer plots dots geom_qq_line() layer plots diagonal line dots less follow.points , bulk data lined nicely. ’s minor outlier, can seen histogram QQ plot.Contrast skewed data. example, Alcohol data set contains per capita consumption (liters) alcohol various countries several years. alcohol consumption variable highly skewed, one can see histogram.also apparent QQ plot data normally distributed.path dots sharply curved, indicating lack normality.","code":"\nggplot(SnowGR, aes(x = Total)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 10, boundary = 50)## Warning: Removed 1 rows containing non-finite values (`stat_bin()`).\nggplot(SnowGR, aes(sample = Total)) +\n    geom_qq() +\n    geom_qq_line()## Warning: Removed 1 rows containing non-finite values (`stat_qq()`).## Warning: Removed 1 rows containing non-finite values (`stat_qq_line()`).\nggplot(Alcohol, aes(x = alcohol)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 2, boundary = 0)\nggplot(Alcohol, aes(sample = alcohol)) +\n    geom_qq() +\n    geom_qq_line()"},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-5a-1","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 5(a)","text":"Find data set numerical variable nearly normal distribution. (can something ’ve already seen past chapter, ’re really ambitious, ’re welcome find new data set.) Plot histogram QQ plot demonstrate data nearly normal. need written response. Just plot graphs.aware use data set package, may add library(PACKAGE) code. (replace word PACKAGE whatever package need.)","code":"\n# Add code here to plot a histogram.\n# Add code here to plot a QQ plot."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"exercise-5b-1","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"Exercise 5(b)","text":"Now find data set numerical variable skewed distribution. Plot histogram QQ plot demonstrate data normal. , need written response. Just plot graphs.","code":"\n# Add code here to plot a histogram.\n# Add code here to plot a QQ plot."},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-conclusion","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.9 Conclusion","text":"normal model ubiquitous statistics, understanding use make predictions critical. certain assumptions met (discussed future chapter), can use normal model make predictions. use z scores allows us measure distances mean terms standard deviations, giving us scale data different contexts comparable long measurements normally distributed. QQ plot helps us check assumption.","code":""},{"path":"normal.html","id":"normal-prep","chapter":"13 Normal models","heading":"13.9.1 Preparing and submitting your assignment","text":"“Run” menu, select “Restart R Run Chunks”.Deal code errors crop . Repeat steps 1–-2 code errors.Spell check document clicking icon “ABC” check mark.Hit “Preview” button one last time generate final draft .nb.html file.Proofread HTML file carefully. errors, go back fix , repeat steps 1–5 .completed chapter part statistics course, follow directions receive professor submit assignment.","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14 Sampling distribution models","text":"\n2.0\n","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"functions-introduced-in-this-chapter-13","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Functions introduced in this chapter","text":"new R functions introduced .","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-intro","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.1 Introduction","text":"chapter, ’ll revisit idea sampling distribution model. ’ve already seen useful can simulate process simulating samples population looking distribution values can occur chance (.e., sampling variability). ’ve also experience working normal models. certain assumptions, can use normal models approximate simulated sampling distributions.","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-install","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.1.1 Install new packages","text":"new packages used chapter.","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-download","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.1.2 Download the R notebook file","text":"Check upper-right corner RStudio make sure ’re intro_stats project. click following link download chapter R notebook file (.Rmd).https://vectorposse.github.io/intro_stats/chapter_downloads/14-sampling_distribution_models.RmdOnce file downloaded, move project folder RStudio open .","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-restart","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.1.3 Restart R and run all chunks","text":"RStudio, select “Restart R Run Chunks” “Run” menu.","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-load","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.2 Load packages","text":"load standard tidyvese package. mosaic package provide coin flips.","code":"\nlibrary(tidyverse)## ── Attaching core tidyverse packages ──────────────────────── tidyverse 2.0.0 ──\n## ✔ dplyr     1.1.2     ✔ readr     2.1.4\n## ✔ forcats   1.0.0     ✔ stringr   1.5.0\n## ✔ ggplot2   3.4.2     ✔ tibble    3.2.1\n## ✔ lubridate 1.9.2     ✔ tidyr     1.3.0\n## ✔ purrr     1.0.2     \n## ── Conflicts ────────────────────────────────────────── tidyverse_conflicts() ──\n## ✖ dplyr::filter() masks stats::filter()\n## ✖ dplyr::lag()    masks stats::lag()\n## ℹ Use the conflicted package (<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>) to force all conflicts to become errors\nlibrary(mosaic)## Warning: package 'mosaic' was built under R version 4.3.1## Registered S3 method overwritten by 'mosaic':\n##   method                           from   \n##   fortify.SpatialPolygonsDataFrame ggplot2\n## \n## The 'mosaic' package masks several functions from core packages in order to add \n## additional features.  The original behavior of these functions should not be affected by this.\n## \n## Attaching package: 'mosaic'\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:Matrix':\n## \n##     mean\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:dplyr':\n## \n##     count, do, tally\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:purrr':\n## \n##     cross\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:ggplot2':\n## \n##     stat\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':\n## \n##     binom.test, cor, cor.test, cov, fivenum, IQR, median, prop.test,\n##     quantile, sd, t.test, var\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:base':\n## \n##     max, mean, min, prod, range, sample, sum"},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-sampling-variability","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.3 Sampling variability and sample size","text":"know sample population, sample “wrong”: even sample representative population, don’t actually expect sample statistic agree exactly population parameter interest. prior simulations demonstrated . centered “true” value (example, hypothesis test, “true” value assumed null value), spread due sampling variability.Let’s explore idea little , time considering sample size plays role sampling variability.Suppose certain candidate election actually 64% support registered voters. conduct poll 10 random people, gathering representative (though large) sample voters.can simulate task R using rflip command mosaic package. Remember default coin flip 50% probability heads, change want model candidate 64% support.can think command taking one random sample size 10 getting certain number “successes”, “success” person votes candidate—encoded “heads”. words, 10 people particular sample, surveyed 6 people said voting candidate 4 people .Using command, can simulate many samples, size 10. Let’s take 1000 samples store variable called sims_1000_10.Note 10 people, impossible get 64% success rate given sample. (6.4 people!) Nevertheless, can see many samples gave us around 5–8 successes, ’d expect true population rate 64%. Also, mean number successes across simulations 6.414, close 6.4.Instead focusing total number successes, let’s use proportion successes sample. can graph simulated proportions, just ’ve done previous chapters. (fancy stuff scale_x_continuous just making sure x-axis goes 0 1 tick marks appear multiples 0.1.)sample size 10, proportion successes can multiples 0.1. Although distribution somewhat normally shaped, discrete (values bars) appreciable left skew.happens increase sample size 20? (binwidth change see discrete bars.)","code":"\nset.seed(13579)\nrflip(10, prob = 0.64)## \n## Flipping 10 coins [ Prob(Heads) = 0.64 ] ...\n## \n## H T H T H H H T T H\n## \n## Number of Heads: 6 [Proportion Heads: 0.6]\nset.seed(13579)\nsims_1000_10 <- do(1000) * rflip(10, prob = 0.64)\nsims_1000_10\nggplot(sims_1000_10, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.05) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 1.1),\n                       breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`).\nset.seed(13579)\nsims_1000_20 <- do(1000) * rflip(20, prob = 0.64)\nggplot(sims_1000_20, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.025) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 1.1),\n                       breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.1))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`)."},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-1-10","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 1","text":"Explain distribution simulations changed going sample size 10 sample size 20.Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-2a-3","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 2(a)","text":"Run set simulations , time samples size 50. Use number simulations (1000) ggplot code (especially scale_x_continuous option) x-axis scaled identically previous cases, change binwidth 0.01.","code":"\nset.seed(13579)\n# Add code here to simulate 1000 random samples of size 50 and plot them."},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-2b-3","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 2(b)","text":"Explain distribution simulations changed going sample size 10 20 50.Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-se","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.4 The sampling distribution model and the standard error","text":"last chapter normal models, mentioned briefly Central Limit Theorem fact certain assumptions, simulations look normally distributed. concretely, Central Limit Theorem tells us sample size increases, distribution sample proportions looks like normal model. model called sampling distribution model describes many different samples population distributed.normal model use? words, mean standard deviation normal model describes simulation repeated samples?simulations centered place, 0.64. surprise. true population proportion 0.64, expect samples around 64% (even , , actually impossible get exactly 64% given sample).standard deviation? seems changing sample size.","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-3-9","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 3","text":"Looking simulations , standard deviation appear change sample size increases? Intuitively, think happens? (Hint: think relationship larger sample sizes accuracy.)Please write answer .standard deviation sampling distribution usually called standard error. (use word “error” statistics mean anyone made mistake. better word error “uncertainty” even just “variability”.)complicated mathematics involved figuring standard error, ’ll just tell . \\(p\\) true population proportion, standard error \\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1 - p)}{n}}.\n\\]Therefore, sample size large enough, sampling distribution model nearly normal, correct normal model \\[\nN\\left(p, \\sqrt{\\frac{p(1 - p)}{n}}\\right).\n\\]election example, can calculate standard error sample size 10:\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1 - p)}{n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.64(1 - 0.64)}{10}} = 0.152.\n\\]can easily using inline R code. (Remember R nothing glorified calculator.) candidate 64% vote take sample size 10, standard error 0.1517893. words, sampling distribution model \\[\nN(0.64, 0.152).\n\\]sample size 20, standard error 0.1073313 sampling distribution model \\[\nN(0.64, 0.107).\n\\]","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-4-8","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 4","text":"Calculate standard error example , time using sample size 50. Give answer contextually meaningful full sentence using inline R code.Please write answer .","code":""},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"samp-dist-models-conditions","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"14.5 Conditions","text":"Like anything statistics, assumptions met applying technique. must check certain conditions true can reasonably make necessary assumptions required model.want use normal model, make sure sampling distribution model truly normal (nearly normal).First, need samples random. Clearly, samples random, danger bias, bets . course, real life hardly sample truly random, representative can usually hope .Second, sample size must less 10% population size. reasons somewhat technical, 10% rough guideline. idea sampling, need sample significant chunk population.two conditions always important sampling. Together, help ensure mathematical assumption independence met. words, two conditions met, better chance data one member sample influence influenced data another member.applying normal models, one condition. called “success/failure” condition. need total number successes least 10 , similarly, total number failures least 10.Go back consider first simulated sample. true rate success population presumed 64%. Given sampling 10 individuals, implies , average, expect 6.4 people 10 vote candidate. likewise, means expect 3.6 people vote candidate. (Clearly, impossible given sample get 6.4 votes , 3.6 votes . average, expect.) fact, since sample size 10, way meet success/failure condition. plotted histogram simulated proportions, saw problem: small numbers, histogram skewed, normal.check success/failure condition calculating \\(np\\) \\(n(1 - p)\\): \\(n\\) sample size \\(p\\) proportion successes. Therefore, \\(np\\) total number successes. Since \\(1 - p\\) proportion failures, \\(n(1 - p)\\) total number failures. numbers \\(np\\) \\(n(1 - p)\\) needs bigger 10.example, \\(n = 10\\) (sample size), \\(p = 0.64\\) (probability success). \\[\nnp = 10(0.64) = 6.4\n\\]\\[\nn(1 - p) = 10(1 - 0.64) = 10(0.36) = 3.6.\n\\]Neither numbers bigger 10.Notice \\(n\\) large, quantities \\(np\\) \\(n(1 - p)\\) also tend large. content Central Limit Theorem: sample sizes grow, sampling distribution model becomes normal.something else going . Suppose \\(n = 100\\) \\(p = 0.01\\). sample seems quite large, let’s look sampling distribution simulation.(Note x-axis scale much smaller .)","code":"\nset.seed(13579)\nsims_1000_100 <- do(1000) * rflip(100, prob = 0.01)\nggplot(sims_1000_100, aes(x = prop)) +\n    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.005) +\n    scale_x_continuous(limits = c(-0.01, 0.1),\n                       breaks = seq(0, 0.1, 0.01))## Warning: Removed 2 rows containing missing values (`geom_bar()`)."},{"path":"samp-dist-models.html","id":"exercise-5-6","chapter":"14 Sampling distribution models","heading":"Exercise 5","text":"’s problem ? Despite fairly large sample size, distribution skewed?Please write answer .scenario, success/failure condition fails 