cts() ──\n## ✖ dplyr::filter() masks stats::filter()\n## ✖ dplyr::lag()    masks stats::lag()\n## ℹ Use the conflicted package (<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>) to force all conflicts to become errors\nlibrary(janitor)## \n## Attaching package: 'janitor'\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':\n## \n##     chisq.test, fisher.test\nlibrary(infer)\nlibrary(openintro)## Loading required package: airports\n## Loading required package: cherryblossom\n## Loading required package: usdata\nlibrary(mosaic)## Warning: package 'mosaic' was built under R version 4.3.1## Registered S3 method overwritten by 'mosaic':\n##   method                           from   \n##   fortify.SpatialPolygonsDataFrame ggplot2\n## \n## The 'mosaic' package masks several functions from core packages in order to add \n## additional features.  The original behavior of these functions should not be affected by this.\n## \n## Attaching package: 'mosaic'\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:Matrix':\n## \n##     mean\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:openintro':\n## \n##     dotPlot\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:infer':\n## \n##     prop_test, t_test\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:dplyr':\n## \n##     count, do, tally\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:purrr':\n## \n##     cross\n## \n## The following object is masked from 'package:ggplot2':\n## \n##     stat\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':\n## \n##     binom.test, cor, cor.test, cov, fivenum, IQR, median, prop.test,\n##     quantile, sd, t.test, var\n## \n## The following objects are masked from 'package:base':\n## \n##     max, mean, min, prod, range, sample, sum"},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-rubric","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.3 Revisiting the rubric for inference","text":"Instead running simulation, going assume sampling distribution can modeled normal model long conditions using normal model met.Although rubric changed, use normal model changes quite bit way go steps. example, won’t simulated values give us histogram null model. Instead, ’ll go straight graphing normal model. won’t compute percent simulated samples least extreme test statistic get P-value. P-value normal model found directly shading model.follows fully-worked example inference one proportion. hypothesis test (sometimes called one-proportion z-test reasons become clear), also follow computing confidence interval. now , consider inference consist hypothesis test confidence interval. Whenever ’re asked question requires statistical inference, follow rubric steps hypothesis test confidence interval.example pause frequently commentary steps, especially execution different ’ve seen used simulation. ’s turn work another example , follow outline rubric, copy paste commentary accompanies .","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-question","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.4 Research question","text":"Data Stanford University Heart Transplant Study located openintro package data frame called heart_transplant. help file learn, “patient entering program designated officially heart transplant candidate, meaning gravely ill likely benefit new heart.” Survival rates good population, although better receive heart transplant. heart transplant recipients still less 50% chance survival?","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-eda","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.5 Exploratory data analysis","text":"","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-documentation","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.5.1 Use data documentation (help files, code books, Google, etc.) to determine as much as possible about the data provenance and structure.","text":"Start typing ?heart_transplant Console searching heart_translplant Help tab read help file.","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"exercise-1-11","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"Exercise 1","text":"Click link “Source” help file. helpful determining provenance data?Now try internet search find original research article 1974. search process also likely help determine provenance data?Please write answer .Now learned everything can reasonably learn data, print look variables.Commentary: variable interest survived, coded factor variable two categories, “alive” “dead”. Keep mind interested survival rates, “alive” condition considered “success” condition.103 patients, considering patients. sample consist patients actually received transplant. following table shows 69 patients “treatment” group (meaning received heart transplant).","code":"\nheart_transplant\nglimpse(heart_transplant)## Rows: 103\n## Columns: 8\n## $ id         <int> 15, 43, 61, 75, 6, 42, 54, 38, 85, 2, 103, 12, 48, 102, 35,…\n## $ acceptyear <int> 68, 70, 71, 72, 68, 70, 71, 70, 73, 68, 67, 68, 71, 74, 70,…\n## $ age        <int> 53, 43, 52, 52, 54, 36, 47, 41, 47, 51, 39, 53, 56, 40, 43,…\n## $ survived   <fct> dead, dead, dead, dead, dead, dead, dead, dead, dead, dead,…\n## $ survtime   <int> 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 16, 16, 16, …\n## $ prior      <fct> no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no,…\n## $ transplant <fct> control, control, control, control, control, control, contr…\n## $ wait       <int> NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, 5, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, …\ntabyl(heart_transplant, transplant) %>%\n    adorn_totals()"},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-prepare","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.5.2 Prepare the data for analysis.","text":"CAUTION: copying pasting example use another research question, following code chunk specific research question applicable contexts.need use filter get patients actually received heart transplant.Commentary: don’t forget double equal sign (==) checks whether treatment variable equal value