ning ’s centered 0.5. work normal model, including checking conditions, must use null model \\(p_{alive}= 0.5\\).’s also numbers don’t whole numbers. null states 69 patients, 50% expected survive, expect 50% 69, 34.5, survive. course, can’t half survivor. actual survivors. Rather, expected number survivors group 69 patients average assumption null.","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-mechanics","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.8 Mechanics","text":"","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-compute-test-stat","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.8.1 Compute the test statistic.","text":"’ll also compute corresponding z score.Commentary: sample proportion code straightforward ’ve seen . get z score, also tell infer null hypothesis knows center normal distribution . hypothesize function, tell infer use “point” null hypothesis p = 0.5. means null specific point: 0.5. (Contrast hypothesis tests two variables null = \"independence\".)can confirm calculation z score manually. ’s easiest compute standard error first. Recall standard error \\[\nSE = \\sqrt{\\frac{p_{alive}(1 - p_{alive})}{n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5(1 - 0.5)}{69}}\n\\]Remember working assumption null hypothesis. means use \\(p_{alive} = 0.5\\) everywhere formula standard error.can math R store result SE.z score \\[\nz = \\frac{(\\hat{p}_{alive} - p_{alive})}{SE} =  \\frac{(\\hat{p}_{alive} - p_{alive})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{p_{alive} (1 - p_{alive})}{n}}} = \\frac{(0.348 - 0.5)}{\\sqrt{\\frac{0.5 (1 - 0.5)}{69}}} =  -2.53.\n\\]Using values alive_prop SE:sample proportion \\(\\hat{p}_{alive}\\) (stored alive_prop) corresponding z-score can considered “test statistic”. use \\(\\hat{p}_{alive}\\) test statistic, ’re considering null model \\[\nN\\left(0.5, \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5 (1 - 0.5)}{69}}\\right).\n\\]use z test statistic, ’re considering null model standard normal model:\\[\nN(0, 1).\n\\]standard normal model intuitive easier work , conceptually R. Generally, , consider z test statistic can consider null model standard normal model. example, knowing test statistic two half standard deviations left null value already tells us lot. can anticipate small P-value leading rejection null. Nevertheless, type hypothesis test, ’ll compute section rubric.","code":"\nalive_prop <- heart_transplant2 %>%\n    specify(response = survived, succes = \"alive\") %>%\n    calculate(stat = \"prop\")\nalive_prop\nalive_z <- heart_transplant2 %>%\n    specify(response = survived, success = \"alive\") %>%\n    hypothesize(null = \"point\", p = 0.5) %>%\n    calculate(stat = \"z\")\nalive_z\nSE <- sqrt(0.5*(1 - 0.5)/69)\nSE## [1] 0.06019293\nz <- (alive_prop - 0.5)/SE\nz"},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-report-test-stat","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.8.2 Report the test statistic in context (when possible).","text":"test statistic 0.3478261. words, 34.7826087% heart transplant recipients alive end study.z score -2.5281029. proportion survivors 2.5 standard errors null value.","code":""},{"path":"one-prop.html","id":"one-prop-ex-plot_null","chapter":"15 Inference for one proportion","heading":"15.8.3 Plot the null distribution.","text":"Commentary: past chapters, used generate verb get many repetitions (usually 1000) kind random process simulate sampling distribution model. chapter, used verb assume instead assume sampling distribution normal model. long conditions hold, reasonable assumption. also means don’t use set.seed random process reproduce.Compare graph see simulated sampling distribution. (Now need set.seed!)essentially picture, although model centered null value 0.5 instead z score 0. also means obs_stat sample proportion alive_prop z score alive_z.","code":"\nalive_test <- heart_transplant2 %>%\n    specify(response = survived, success = \"alive\") %>%\n    hypothesize(null = \"point\", p = 0.5) %>%\n    assume(distribution = \"z\")\nalive_test## A Z distribution.\nalive_test %>%\n  