females), 29 50 (successes failures among males). larger 10.\nChecking contingency table (one counts), see numbers 28 98 (successes failures among females), 29 50 (successes failures among males). larger 10.Commentary: Ideally, success/failure condition like check \\(n_{1} p_{1}\\), \\(n_{1} (1 - p_{1})\\), \\(n_{2} p_{2}\\), \\(n_{2} (1 - p_{2})\\); however, null makes claim values \\(p_{1}\\) \\(p_{2}\\). next best thing estimate substituting sample proportions \\(\\hat{p}_{1}\\) \\(\\hat{p}_{2}\\). \\(n_{1} \\hat{p}_{1}\\) \\(n_{2} \\hat{p}_{2}\\) just raw counts successes group. Likewise, \\(n_{1} (1 - \\hat{p}_{1})\\) \\(n_{2} (1 - \\hat{p}_{2})\\) just raw counts failures group. ’s can just read contingency table.sophisticated approach, one also use “pooled proportions”. See optional appendix chapter information.","code":""},{"path":"two-prop.html","id":"mechanics-3","chapter":"16 Inference for two proportions","heading":"16.9 Mechanics","text":"","code":""},{"path":"two-prop.html","id":"compute-the-test-statistic.-3","chapter":"16 Inference for two proportions","heading":"16.9.1 Compute the test statistic.","text":"test statistic difference proportions sample, \\(\\hat{p}_{died, F} - \\hat{p}_{died, M}\\):\\[\n\\hat{p}_{died, F} - \\hat{p}_{died, M} = 0.222 - 0.367 = -0.145\n\\]z-score:Commentary: can confirm value z-score manually just make sure understand comes .standard error looks like following:\\[\nSE = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat{p}_{died, F} (1 - \\hat{p}_{died, F})}{n_{F}} + \\frac{\\hat{p}_{died, M} (1 - \\hat{p}_{died, M})}{n_M}}\n\\]Plugging numbers exploratory data analysis output:\\[\nSE = \\sqrt{\\frac{0.222 (1 - 0.222)}{126} + \\frac{0.367 (1 - 0.367)}{79}}\n\\]R,Now z-score formula \\[\nz = \\frac{(\\hat{p}_{died, F} - \\hat{p}_{died, M}) - (p_{died, F} - p_{died, M})}{SE}\n\\]first term numerator \\((\\hat{p}_{died, F} - \\hat{p}_{died, M})\\) test statistic, -0.145. second term numerator \\((p_{died, F} - p_{died, M})\\) zero according null hypothesis. Plugging , along value SE, gives\\[\nz = \\frac{-0.145 - 0}{0.066} \\approx -2.2\n\\]little rounding error (since rounded everything sight three decimal places instead keeping precision), infer output also reported.","code":"\nobs_diff <- Melanoma %>%\n    observe(status_fct ~ sex_fct, success = \"died from melanoma\",\n            stat = \"diff in props\", order = c(\"female\", \"male\"))\nobs_diff\nobs_diff_z <- Melanoma %>%\n    observe(status_fct ~ sex_fct, success = \"died from melanoma\",\n            stat = \"z\", order = c(\"female\", \"male\"))\nobs_diff_z\nsqrt(0.222 * (1 - 0.222) / 126 + 0.367 * (1 - 0.367) / 79)## [1] 0.06566131"},{"path":"two-prop.html","id":"report-the-test-statistic-in-context-when-possible.-3","chapter":"16 Inference for two proportions","heading":"16.9.2 Report the test statistic in context (when possible).","text":"sample, -14.4866385% difference rate women men Denmark die malignant melanoma (meaning males died higher rate).test statistic z score -2.2530721. difference proportions rate women men Denmark die malignant melanoma lies bit 2 standard errors left null value.Commentary: Note phrase “meaning males died higher rate”. looking difference, must indicate direction difference. Without , know difference, idea whether women men die malignant melanoma. know subtracting female minus male, given values negative, can infer males die malignant melanoma often females samples.","code":""},{"path":"two-prop.html","id":"plot-the-null-distribution.-3","chapter":"16 Inference for two proportions","heading":"16.9.3 Plot the null distribution.","text":"Commentary: Remember two-sided test.red line location test statistic, tails shaded count toward P-value.","code":"\nstatus_sex_test <- Melanoma %>%\n    specify(status_fct ~ sex_fct, success = \"died from melanoma\") %>%\n    hypothesize(null = \"independence\") %>%\n    assume(distribution = \"z\")\nstatus_sex_test## A Z distribution.\nstatus_sex_test %>%\n    visualize() +\n    shade_p_va