ol one single group calculate successes whole group.called pooled proportion. ’s straightforward compute: just take total number successes groups divide total size groups. formula:\\[\\hat{p}_{pooled} = \\frac{successes_{1} + successes_{2}}{n_{1} + n_{2}}.\\]Occasionally, given raw number successes group, rather, proportion successes group, \\(\\hat{p}_{1}\\) \\(\\hat{p}_{2}\\). simple fix recompute raw count successes \\(n_{1} \\hat{p}_{1}\\) \\(n_{2} \\hat{p}_{2}\\). looks like formula:\\[\\hat{p}_{pooled} = \\frac{n_{1} \\hat{p}_{1} + n_{2} \\hat{p}_{2}}{n_{1} + n_{2}}.\\]\nnormal model can still mean \\(p_{1} - p_{2}\\). (usually assume 0 null hypothesis.) standard error use pooled proportion:\\[N\\left( p_{1} - p_{2}, \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat{p}_{pooled} (1 - \\hat{p}_{pooled})}{n_{1}} + \\frac{\\hat{p}_{pooled} (1 - \\hat{p}_{pooled})}{n_{2}}} \\right).\\]can use pooled proportion standard error, fact can use anywhere assume null. example, success/failure condition also subject assumption null, use pooled proportion .confidence interval, things different. null hypothesis effect computing confidence interval, assumption justify pooling.standard error one-proportion interval \\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat{p}(1 - \\hat{p})}{n}}\n\\]just substitutes \\(\\hat{p}\\) \\(p\\). standard error two-proportion case:\\[\nSE = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat{p}_{1} (1 - \\hat{p}_{1})}{n_{1}} + \\frac{\\hat{p}_{2} (1 - \\hat{p}_{2})}{n_{2}}}.\n\\]","code":""},{"path":"chi-square-goodness-of-fit-test.html","id":"chi-square-goodness-of-fit-test","chapter":"17 Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","heading":"17 Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","text":"\n2.0\n","code":""},{"path":"chi-square-goodness-of-fit-test.html","id":"functions-introduced-in-this-chapter-16","chapter":"17 Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","heading":"Functions introduced in this chapter:","text":"chisq.test","code":""},{"path":"chi-square-goodness-of-fit-test.html","id":"introduction-1","chapter":"17 Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","h